---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import { PAGE_HERO_IMAGES } from "../consts";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Medicolegal Fundamentals | Radiology & Medicolegal Law"
  description="The core principles of UK clinical negligence law applied to radiology: duty, breach, causation, consent and expert evidence."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Medicolegal fundamentals"
    title="Medicolegal Fundamentals"
    description="The legal principles that govern radiological practice and clinical negligence."
    heroImage={PAGE_HERO_IMAGES["/medicolegal-fundamentals"] || undefined}
  />

  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Core legal framework"
    paragraphs={[
      "To establish clinical negligence in radiology, a claimant must prove that a duty of care was breached, and that this breach directly caused actionable harm.",
      "This page sets out foundational legal principles. Detailed legislation, professional standards, and recent case law are explored separately.",
    ]}
    contents={[
      { href: "#framework", label: "The legal framework" },
      { href: "#authorities", label: "Core authorities" },
      { href: "#radiology", label: "Application to radiology" },
      { href: "#nhs-data", label: "NHS and claims data" },
      { href: "#expert-evidence", label: "Expert evidence" },
    ]}
    audience="Written for radiologists, lawyers, NHS legal teams and expert witnesses."
    reviewed="October 2026"
  />

  <div class="container-page space-y-6 band">
    <Figure
      label="Figure 01"
      caption="The structure of a clinical negligence claim"
      subtitle="Duty, breach, causation and damage must be considered separately."
    >
      <Diagram name="legal-questions" />
    </Figure>
  </div>

  <!-- PART I: LEGAL FRAMEWORK -->

  <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #64748b;">
    <span class="part-accent"></span>

    <div class="part-mark">
      <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
      <span class="part-mark-label">
        Part I
        <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Legal framework</span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <Section
      id="framework"
      eyebrow="Part I"
      title="The Elements of a Clinical Negligence Claim"
    >
      <p class="para">
        An adverse clinical outcome, reporting discrepancy, or missed
        abnormality does not automatically establish negligence. On the balance
        of probabilities, the claimant must prove duty, breach, causation, and
        compensable harm.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Duty</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            A radiologist ordinarily assumes a duty of care when accepting
            responsibility for an examination, procedure, or report.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Breach</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            The core test is whether the radiologist exercised the reasonable
            skill and care expected of a competent practitioner undertaking that
            work at the time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Causation</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            The claimant must demonstrate that, but for the breach, the injury
            or materially worse outcome would probably have been avoided.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Damage</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Negligence is not actionable without injury or loss. A reporting
            error with no clinical consequence will not support an award of
            damages.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </div>

  <!-- PART II: CORE AUTHORITIES -->

  <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #475569;">
    <span class="part-accent"></span>

    <div class="part-mark">
      <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
      <span class="part-mark-label">
        Part II
        <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Core authorities</span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <Section id="authorities" eyebrow="Part II" title="Key Legal Precedents">
      <p class="para">
        Established case law provides the essential framework for professional
        standards, perceptual versus evaluative errors, causation, loss of
        chance, and informed consent.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="bolam"
      eyebrow="Standard of care"
      title="Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee"
      citation="[1957] 1 WLR 582"
    >
      <p class="para">
        Professional practice is not negligent if it accords with a responsible
        body of medical opinion.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            The law acknowledges a range of acceptable professional practice. A
            clinician is not negligent merely because a peer would have acted
            differently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Divergent interpretations often fall within a permitted range of
            practice. Liability turns on whether original reporting fell outside
            responsible standards, not simply whether an abnormality can be seen
            retrospectively.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="bolitho"
      eyebrow="Logical scrutiny"
      title="Bolitho v City and Hackney Health Authority"
      citation="[1998] AC 232"
    >
      <p class="para">
        Professional opinion must withstand logical analysis; courts are not
        bound to accept expert testimony simply because a body of practitioners
        supports it.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Supporting opinion must be reasonable, reasoned, and capable of
            balancing material risks and benefits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Experts must explain why a report was defensible given the images,
            protocol, clinical context, and standards of the era. Assertion
            alone is insufficient.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="muller"
      eyebrow="Breach: Visible vs. Demonstrable"
      title="Muller v King's College Hospital NHS Foundation Trust"
      citation="[2017] EWHC 128 (QB)"
    >
      <p class="para">
        In pure diagnostic and interpretation cases (such as radiology,
        histology, and ECGs), the traditional <em class="italic">Bolam</em> deference
        to a "responsible body of opinion" has restricted application when deciding
        whether an anomaly was visible.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            The court must determine as a matter of fact whether what is visible
            on the image or slide demonstrates a clear reporting error,
            distinguishing objective perceptual omissions from matters of
            genuine clinical judgment or opinion.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            A failure to identify a plain, conspicuous abnormality falls outside
            acceptable standards of care, regardless of whether a minority of
            peers might miss it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="barnett"
      eyebrow="Factual causation"
      title="Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital Management Committee"
      citation="[1969] 1 QB 428"
    >
      <p class="para">
        A breach establishes liability only if it actively caused the injury,
        evaluated through the conventional “but for” test.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            If the injury would likely have occurred regardless of the breach,
            factual causation is not established.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Identifying a missed finding is insufficient; the claimant must
            prove that a competent report would have prompted action preventing
            or reducing the harm.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="gregg"
      eyebrow="Loss of chance"
      title="Gregg v Scott"
      citation="[2005] UKHL 2"
    >
      <p class="para">
        English law rejects the doctrine of "loss of a chance" for
        medical-negligence claims concerning physical injury and reduced life
        expectancy.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Principle</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            A claimant must prove on a balance of probabilities (greater than
            50%) that a delayed diagnosis caused a worse outcome, rather than
            simply proving that a delay reduced their statistical chance of
            survival.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            In oncology delays, proving that a missed tumour reduced survival
            odds from 51% to 40% succeeds, whereas a drop from 45% to 30% fails
            because the baseline probability of survival was already below 50%.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="montgomery"
      eyebrow="Consent"
      title="Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board"
      citation="[2015] UKSC 11"
    >
      <p class="para">
        Clinicians must take reasonable care to ensure patients are informed of
        the material risks of recommended treatments and any reasonable
        alternatives.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Materiality</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            A risk is material if a reasonable person in the patient's position
            would attach significance to it, or if the clinician should
            reasonably know that this specific patient would do so.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Radiology</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            This duty is critical in interventional procedures, requiring
            consent discussions tailored to the individual patient regarding
            material risks and options.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Figure
      label="Figure 02"
      caption="The foundational authorities"
      subtitle="Professional standard, logical scrutiny, visible error, causation, loss of chance, and consent."
    >
      <Diagram name="case-timeline" />
    </Figure>
  </div>

  <!-- PART III: APPLICATION TO RADIOLOGY -->

  <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #334155;">
    <span class="part-accent"></span>

    <div class="part-mark">
      <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
      <span class="part-mark-label">
        Part III
        <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Application to radiology</span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <Section
      id="radiology"
      eyebrow="Part III"
      title="Applying Legal Principles to Clinical Practice"
    >
      <p class="para">
        Assessing radiological negligence requires more than demonstrating
        retrospective visibility; it demands careful reconstruction of the
        original reporting context.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Original study</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Review the complete source examination, including protocols, image
            quality, anatomical coverage, and relevant prior studies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Clinical context</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Establish the referral information and clinical questions available
            to the original reporter at the time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Interpretation</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Distinguish a perceptual oversight from an abnormality that was
            detected but reasonably interpreted differently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Communication</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Verify whether urgent or unexpected findings were appropriately
            escalated, communicated, and logged through proper pathways.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Causation</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Determine what a competent report would have changed in terms of
            follow-up imaging, referral, intervention, and ultimate outcome.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </div>

  <!-- PART IV: NHS AND CLAIMS DATA -->

  <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #1e293b;">
    <span class="part-accent"></span>

    <div class="part-mark">
      <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
      <span class="part-mark-label">
        Part IV
        <span class="part-mark-label-sub">NHS and claims data</span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <Section id="nhs-data" eyebrow="Part IV" title="NHS Claims Context">
      <p class="para">
        NHS Resolution publishes national data detailing clinical negligence
        claims, expenditure, and resolution methods. These figures reflect
        systemic claims activity rather than the absolute incidence of clinical
        error.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">National position</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            In 2025/26, NHS Resolution received 15,236 new clinical negligence
            claims and reported incidents. Eighty-four per cent of claims were
            resolved without formal proceedings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Interpreting the data</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            Notifications, settlements, payments, and financial provisions
            measure different metrics. A claim notification does not equate to a
            judicial finding of negligence.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="para-sm">
        Source: NHS Resolution, <em>Annual Report and Accounts 2025/26</em>.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section
      eyebrow="Radiology evidence"
      title="Incidental Findings in Abdominal Radiology Claims"
    >
      <p class="para">
        A retrospective review by five abdominal-radiology expert witnesses
        examined 112 medicolegal cases comprising 176 individual examinations.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Findings</h3>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>
              Forty-one of 112 allegations (37%) involved incidental pathology.
            </li>
            <li>
              Suspected malignancy represented the most frequent incidental
              finding allegation.
            </li>
            <li>
              Twelve of the 41 incidental interpretations were judged
              substandard.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Limitations</h3>
          <p class="para-sm">
            This analysis represents a selected expert-witness cohort rather
            than a national dataset, and should not be generalized to estimate
            total claim distributions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="para-sm">
        Source: Halligan S, Blunt D, Boone D, Higginson T, and Plumb AA,
        <em>Clinical Radiology</em>, 2026;92:107162.
      </p>
    </Section>
  </div>

  <!-- PART V: EXPERT EVIDENCE -->

  <div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #0f172a;">
    <span class="part-accent"></span>

    <div class="part-mark">
      <span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
      <span class="part-mark-label">
        Part V
        <span class="part-mark-label-sub">Expert evidence</span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <Section
      id="expert-evidence"
      eyebrow="Part V"
      title="A Defensible Expert Approach"
    >
      <p class="para">
        Medical experts owe an overriding duty to assist the court rather than
        the instructing party. Opinions must clearly distinguish retrospective
        visibility from prospective standards of care, and critically assess
        whether a competent report would have altered the clinical outcome.
      </p>

      <div class="entry-list">
        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Breach</h3>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Examine original source images where available.</li>
            <li>
              Record technical parameters, image quality, and study limitations.
            </li>
            <li>
              Define precisely what information was available to the original
              reporter.
            </li>
            <li>Review prior imaging in strict chronological sequence.</li>
            <li>
              Assess objectively whether the report fell outside responsible
              practice.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="entry">
          <h3 class="entry-title">Causation</h3>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>State what a competent report should have contained.</li>
            <li>Identify the probable clinical response to that report.</li>
            <li>
              Isolate radiological opinions from other specialist medical
              evidence.
            </li>
            <li>
              Avoid assuming an earlier diagnosis guaranteed an improved
              clinical outcome.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Callout tone="caution" title="Scope">
      <p>
        This page provides an educational summary of foundational principles.
        Detailed legislation, professional standards, recent case law, duty of
        candour, and AI-assisted reporting are addressed separately. It does not
        constitute legal advice or replace primary source authorities.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
